[Music]  hello everyone so today i'd like to  share with you all some things that i've  been working on at the piano regarding  contrary motion i've written three  exercises that are increasing in  complexity from one to three but before  i do that i'd like to share with you all  some tips that i keep in mind regarding  the layout of the keys and also some  things about outlining  so i hope you enjoy it so quite simply  looking at the layout of the keys we see  three black keys followed by two  three two three we're going to first  focus on the outer white notes  of the groups of three  so let's take our middle three fingers  and place them on the group of three  black keys  then our thumbs will be in place for  the outer notes and we're just going to  be using our thumbs so we're basically  playing either an f or b  [Music]  next we're going to add in the groups of  two notes which we'll be using  these two fingers  so now let's just do the black keys  [Music]  next add in the f's and the b's what we  were playing with our thumbs  [Music]  and the reason why i use f sharp major  is because there is a very obvious  physical layout and visual layout that  you can easily grab onto  [Music]  now when you get into other types of  patterns and different scales such as c  lydian  what happens in contrary motion is not  like the f sharp major scale where  it's in symmetry  so for example if we start on  the third degree and go outwards  [Music]  the black keys don't happen at the same  time which can throw you off so now  we're going to broaden the scope of how  we organize each little group  [Music]  so here's a simple pattern it sounds  like this  now with both hands  [Music]  so there are many ways to look at this  but i'm going to be looking at it in  three parts part one  part two  and then part three is also  c d e f sharp g  and that's an easy way to remember this  [Music]  now let's move on to a slightly more  complicated pattern  [Music]  so again let's divide it into three  parts first of all these five notes  again  and now here it's a little different  because in the right hand we have a  second inversion  major triad  [Music]  and then the left hand we have a first  inversion minor triad  and the last part  of course the notes are different than  the first exercise that i just showed  but the grouping in my head is the same  i'm still thinking about this as these  five notes  i'm thinking of the pattern  three one two five  so that i'm not thrown off by the black  key here and the black key with my  fourth finger  so again  [Music]  now we're going to move on to a more  complicated pattern and in this one  unlike the other ones i'm not going to  be mirroring the hands in an exact way  and i will be explaining why  [Music]  so the pattern consists of 10 notes  which are then repeated  so if we're going to be mirroring our  hands in an exact way following the  notes of  c lydian it would sound like this  [Music]  which sounds okay but in my opinion it  could sound a little better so in  looking at this pattern going inwards we  first start with b's on  each end  that sounds okay but again i don't like  it because  the c lydian sound is not as strongly  implied because of this b base  so i want to change that so we're going  to  start with the c so now after deciding  to start with the c in the left hand  i'm just going to be composing a counter  line to this line going backwards  and that's how i came up with  now with a pattern like this what i'm  latching onto is not as  neatly organized as the three groups for  example of the last exercises  so for this specific exercise the first  group that i'm thinking of is a little  bit broad  it's this group here but i'm focusing  mainly on the octaves  [Music]  after that i'm focusing on these lines  [Music]  and in addition to that i'm focusing on  the d and the c  [Music]  and i like that because it's a little  offset  and my ears latch onto that  [Music]  now i'm going in the opposite direction  the groups that i'm thinking of remain  the same  [Music]  [Music]  by the way i've written about five other  sets of these exercises using different  modes but i'd like to add just a few  more before i release the pdf anyway  thanks so much for watching and i'll see  you in the next video  [Music]  you

